# Multimode Emission from Lanthanide‐Based Metal–Organic Frameworks for Advanced Information Encryption

## abstract
Although remarkable progress on luminescent materials is made in advanced optical information storage and anti‐counterfeiting applications, many challenges still remain in these fields. Currently, most luminescent materials are based on a single photoluminescent model that can be easily imitated by substitutes. In this work, a series of multimodal emission lanthanide‐based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are developed, where they emit red and green light originating from Eu3  and Tb3  under ultraviolet light irradiation. Meanwhile, under 980 nm near‐infrared laser irradiation, these MOFs show cyan upconversion cooperative luminescence derived from Yb3  and characteristic upconversion luminescence from lanthanide activators (Eu3 , Tb3 , or Ho3 ), respectively. Based on the integrated optical functionality, the functional information storage applications are successfully designed, which indicates that multimodal emission features can be easily detected under ultraviolet lamps (254 or 393 nm) or 980 nm near‐infrared laser. And, the unique optical features show a high level of security in the advanced information storage application, which would be sufficiently complex to be forged.

Although remarkable progress on luminescent materials is made in advanced optical information storage and anti-counterfeiting applications, many challenges still remain in these fields. Currently, most luminescent materials are based on a single photoluminescent model that can be easily imitated by substitutes. In this work, a series of multimodal emission lanthanide-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are developed, where they emit red and green light originating from $\mathsf{E}\mathsf{u}^{3+}$ and $\bar{\mathsf{T}}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ under ultraviolet light irradiation. Meanwhile, under ${\mathfrak{s}}80\ {\mathfrak{n}}{\mathfrak{m}}$ near-infrared laser irradiation, these MOFs show cyan upconversion cooperative luminescence derived from $\yen6^{3+}$ and characteristic upconversion luminescence from lanthanide activators $(\mathsf{E}\mathsf{u}^{3+}$ , $\bar{\mathsf{T}}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , or ${\mathsf{H o}}^{3+}$ ), respectively. Based on the integrated optical functionality, the functional information storage applications are successfully designed, which indicates that multimodal emission features can be easily detected under ultraviolet lamps (254 or $393~\mathsf{n m}]$ ) or $\mathsf{\pmb{980}}\mathsf{n m}$ near-infrared laser. And, the unique optical features show a high level of security in the advanced information storage application, which would be sufficiently complex to be forged.
Photoluminescent materials do respond to their specific excitation light. Unfortunately, most photoluminescent materials only respond to one excitation light, in which case a single color is always easy to decipher. Some special photoluminescent materials are able to respond to multiple excitation wavelengths. It has been demonstrated in a number of studies that exploiting multimode-emission fluorescent materials is essential to promote information security.[3] And, multi-dimensional optical information storage can be achieved from a single material by varying the excitation wavelength, which improves the storage density and data security.
Trivalent lanthanide ions have been widely used to establish multimodeemission platforms because of their special 4f electron orbital structure and abundant energy levels.[4] For instance, $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions have been extensively used in the development of green and red down-shifting emission luminescent materials.[5] $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ ,
## 1. Introduction
With the rapid growth of digital information, the storage and encryption of massive digital information is a serious challenge. $\mathrm{Op}$ tical information storage is an emerging, promising, and highly secure storage method.[1] Photoluminescent materials are widely used in data recording, storage and security technologies due to their relatively short response time and variable excitation modes.[2]
${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ , and other lanthanide ions can absorb specific nearinfrared (NIR) light for sensitizing upconversion emission.[6] Among the numerous multimode luminescent lanthanidedoped fluorescent materials, lanthanide-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention because of their unique framework structures, variable pore channels, and high stability.[7] The organic ligands of MOFs usually have strong absorption in the ultra-violet (UV) region, so they can be employed for energy transfer, resulting in efficient down-shifting emission of lanthanide ions. However, the upconversion luminescence excited by NIR light is rarely found in multimode luminescent MOFs.[8] The upconversion cooperative luminescence in lanthanide-based MOFs has been reported in our previous work, which makes it possible to develop a series of multimode-emission lanthanide-based MOFs with upconversion emission.[9]
In this work, 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (BTC) is served as an organic linker and lanthanide ions such as ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ho}^{3+}$ are used as metal centers. A series of lanthanide-based MOFs with multimode emission are developed. The $497\mathrm{nm}$ upconversion cooperative luminescence of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and the characteristic upconversion emission of the lanthanide activators itself are both generated under $980~\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation. By altering the type and concentration of doping lanthanide ions, the ratio of cooperative luminescence of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ to the characteristic emission of lanthanide activators will be modulated to display different colors. In addition, the red emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and green emission of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ can be achieved under excitation of UV light as well (Scheme 1). In proof-of-concept experiments, these multimode-emission lanthanide-based MOFs are successfully applied for information storage applications. It provides a new perspective for achieving high-density and highly secure information storage.
## 2. Results and Discussion
BTC and ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ ions can be assembled into an organized framework structure (Yb-BTC MOFs). In previous studies, it has been known that one ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ ion is coordinated with seven oxygen atoms in the framework, six of which are from BTC ligands and one from water.[10] The crystalline cell forms a long chain along the $c$ -axis (Figure 1a), which is interconnected by the phenyl groups of $a$ -axis and $b$ -axis to form a 3D framework (Figure 1b). Then various multimode-emission MOFs are obtained by doping different lanthanide activators to replace the ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ ions. As an example, a series of Eu-doped multimode-emission MOFs (denoted as x%Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs, $x=1$ , 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50) were prepared after different concentrations of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions were doped. The X-ray diffraction patterns of $x\%\mathrm{Eu,Yb}$ -BTC MOFs have no obvious change with increase of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration (Figure 1c), and the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of x%Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs also demonstrate that their morphologies do not change obviously (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The Fourier transform infrared spectra, absorption spectra, and thermal stability of MOFs doped with different concentrations of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ exhibit similar characteristics (Figures S2–S4, Supporting Information). These results indicate that the original structure of MOFs is not changed significantly after the lanthanide activators are doped.
The elemental distribution and content of the $10\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs are shown in Figure 1d and Figure S5, Supporting Information. They directly demonstrate that the Eu elements are uniformly doped in $10\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs. According to the result of photostability test, the $10\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs maintain longterm luminescence stability under $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation with a maximum power density of $10\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Therefore, the upconversion luminescence spectra of $x\%\mathrm{Eu}$ ,Yb-BTC MOFs were recorded at the power density of $10\ \mathrm{W\cm}^{-2}$ for $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation. As shown in Figure 1e, the $x\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs exhibit two main emission bands at 497 and $615~\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation with $980~\mathrm{nm}$ laser. The emission at 497 nm originates from the cooperative luminescence of the Yb–Yb dimers,[9,11] while the emission at $615\mathrm{nm}$ is derived from the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ transition of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ (Figure 1f).[12] As demonstrated in Figure S7, Supporting Information, the cooperative luminescence from ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and the $615\mathrm{nm}$ upconversion emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ are both two-photon upconversion processes.
The intensity of 497 and $615~\mathrm{nm}$ emission bands is altered with the change of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration in the frameworks (Figure ${\bf\Xi}_{1}\ {\bf\Lambda}_{\bf g})$ . The $497~\mathrm{nm}$ emission intensity decreases with the rise of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions concentration, which is attributed to the direct correlation between the intensity of cooperative luminescence and the ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ concentration. The lower ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ concentration means that the formation of Yb–Yb dimers in MOFs is less likely. The intensity of $615~\mathrm{nm}$ emission increases and then decreases with ascending ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration, reaching the maximum when the doping concentration of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ is $10\%$ . When the concentration of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ is low (below $10\%$ ), the main factor affecting the luminescence intensity is the amount of activators, thus the emission intensity of $615\ \mathrm{nm}$ rises with the increase of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ concentration. When the concentration of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ is high (above $10\%$ ), the main factor affecting the luminescence intensity is the amount of sensitizers. The increase of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration represents the decrease of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ concentration in the framework, which causes the emission intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ at $615\ \mathrm{nm}$ is weakened.[13] The ratio of the emission intensity at 615 and $497\ \mathrm{nm}$ increases along with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration rising, which demonstrates that the upconversion emission of $x\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs is gradually dominated by the upconversion emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ (Figure $\lvert1\textup{h}$ ). The ratio change simultaneously leads to consequent alteration of the upconversion luminescence color of the x%Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs under $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Figure S8, Supporting Information), from cyan region shifting to red region with increasing ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration, displaying in the Commission Internationale de l’Eclairage (CIE) chromaticity diagram as well (Figure S9, Supporting Information).
The x%Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs not only produce upconversion luminescence under $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, but also display efficient down-shifting emission. Excitation spectra of $x\%\mathrm{Eu,Yb}$ BTC MOFs were shown in Figure 2a monitored by $615\mathrm{nm}$ emission. The broad absorption band in the range of $225{-}320~\mathrm{nm}$ originates from the absorption of BTC ligands, the transition from ground state to the first excited singlet state $(\mathsf{S}_{1})$ .[14] The narrow absorption band at $393~\mathrm{nm}$ originates from the absorption of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions $(^{5}\mathrm{L}_{6}\leftarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0})$ .[15] As shown in Figure 2b, the energy transfers from the ${^5}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ state to ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ state via non-radiative relaxation, leading to the emission of $615~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ through the $\mathrm{{}^{5}D}_{0}\rightarrow\mathrm{{}^{7}F}_{2}$ transition. The absorption cross section of the BTC ligand is much larger than that of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, so that the red luminescence of the $x\%\mathrm{Eu},\mathrm{Yb}$ -BTC MOFs is stronger under $254~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation than $393{\mathrm{nm}}$ excitation (Figure S10, Supporting Information). From Figure 2a, it can be observed that the intensity of the broad absorption band $(225-320\:\mathrm{nm}$ ) of the ligand increases and then decreases upon the rise of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration (Figure S11, Supporting Information) with a maximum at $20\%$ , so that the emission intensity of x%Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs also has the same trend under $254\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Figure 2c,e). This is attributed to a concentration quenching effect at high $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ concentrations.[16] Upon increasing the $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ concentration, a significant lengthening was observed for lifetime of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ state $(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}$ $=615\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) under $254~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, as shown in Figure S12 and Table S1, Supporting Information. However, the intensity of the narrow absorption band of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ (at $393\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) increases with rise of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration (Figure S11, Supporting Information). And, the emission intensity of $x\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs also enhances with increasing ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration under $393~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Figure 2d,f). Meanwhile, the lifetime of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ state also prolongs significantly with the increase of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=393\mathrm{~nm}$ , Figure S13 and Table S2, Supporting Information). The concentration quenching effect still exists, while the strongest luminescence intensity of $x\%$ Eu,Yb-BTC MOFs is at $50\%$ mol ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ concentration in the dosages range studied. This may be owing to the fact that the concentration of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ has not yet reached the threshold of concentration quenching effect under $393{\mathrm{nm}}$ excitation.
When $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions are used to replace ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions as activators, a series of $x\%\mathrm{{rb}}$ ,Yb-BTC MOFs $(x=1$ , 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50) are produced (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Similar multimode-emission phenomena are also observed in the $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs, which exhibit two major emission bands at 497 (from ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ ) and $545~\mathrm{nm}$ (from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ) upon $980~\mathrm{nm}$ laser excitation (Figure 3a). The emission band at $545~\mathrm{nm}$ originates from the $\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}F_{5}}$ transition of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions (Figure 3b).[17] The $497~\mathrm{nm}$ emission intensity reduces, while the emission intensity at $545\mathrm{nm}$ enhances with the increase of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ concentration (Figure 3c). The increase of the concentration of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ results in a progressive predominance of green emission (Figure 3d and Figure S15, Supporting Information) and the luminescent color also progressively shifts toward green in the CIE chromaticity diagram in x%Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs (Figure S16, Supporting Information).
As expected, under $254\mathrm{nm}$ excitation the $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=1$ , 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50) exhibit efficient down-shifting green emission. The BTC ligands absorb UV excitation light to reach $\mathsf{S}_{1}$ excited state and the energy is then transferred to the triplet $(\mathrm{T}_{1})$ excited state through intersystem crossing. And subsequently the excitation energy is transferred to the $\mathrm{^5D_{4}}$ state of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions via energy transfer (ET), ultimately resulting in the characterized $545\mathrm{nm}$ emission band (attributed to the ${}^{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{D}_{4}}\ensuremath{\rightarrow}^{7}\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}_{5}}$ transition) (Figure 4a,b). The excitation spectra of $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs indicates that the broad absorption band of $225{-}320~\mathrm{nm}$ originating from BTC enhances with the increasing $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ concentration (Figure 4c), the same trend as that of emission intensity at $545~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 4d). As shown in Figure S17 and Table S3, Supporting Information, the lifetime of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ at the $^5\mathrm{D}_{4}$ state $(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}$ $=545\mathrm{nm}$ ) increases slowly with the increasing concentration of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ in the frameworks, which indicates that the $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs are also slightly affected by the concentration quenching effect in the luminescence process. The $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs exhibit bright green emission even if the concentration of $\mathrm{T}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ ions is as low as $1\%$ (under $254~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, Figure S18, Supporting Information). The position of the down-shifting luminescent color of $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs is almost constant on the CIE chromaticity diagram under $254~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Figure S19, Supporting Information). This demonstrates that the downshifting emission of $x\%$ Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs is efficient and stable.
In addition to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ ions are also classical lanthanide activators for upconversion luminescence. A series of $x\%\mathrm{{Ho}}$ ,Yb-BTC MOFs $(x=1$ , 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50) are prepared and used for upconversion luminescence investigation (Figure S20, Supporting Information). Similarly, under $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, the upconversion luminescence spectra of $x\%H_{0}$ ,Yb-BTC
MOFs also exhibit cooperative luminescence at $497~\mathrm{nm}$ (from $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+})$ ) and a red emission at $660~\mathrm{nm}$ from ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ ions that sensitized by the ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ (Figure 5a). The electrons are pumped to the $^5\mathrm{F}_{4}$ state of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ through energy transfer upconversion, resulting in the $660\mathrm{nm}$ emission via the $^5\mathrm{F}_{5}\rightarrow{}^{5}\mathrm{I}_{8}$ transition.[18] Compared with Eu,Yb-BTC and Tb,Yb-BTC MOFs mentioned above, the 497 nm cooperative luminescence of Ho,Yb-BTC MOFs reduces rapidly with the increase of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ concentration. The $497~\mathrm{nm}$ emission is almost not able to be observed at all when concentration of doped ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ ions is greater than $40\%$ . This is attributed to the fact that the ${}^{5}\mathrm{I}_{6}$ state of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ can perform efficient resonant energy transfer with the $^2\mathrm{F}_{5/2}$ state of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ . The energy population at the $^2\mathrm{F}_{5/2}$ state of ${\mathrm{Y}}{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}$ decays rapidly with increasing ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ concentration, resulting in that the cooperative luminescence cannot be observed at higher ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ concentration $(\geq$ $40\%$ .[19] The emission peak at $660~\mathrm{nm}$ first increases and then decreases with enhancing ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ concentration, with a maximum at $10\%$ doping. Similar to the upconversion emission of Eu,YbBTC MOFs, the amount of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ determines the intensity of the emission at $660\mathrm{nm}$ when the concentration of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ is low. The decrease in the amount of sensitizer ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ leads to a weakening of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ emission intensity when the ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ ions concentration is higher $(>10\%)$ . The colors of the upconversion luminescence change significantly with the alteration of $\mathrm{Ho}^{3+}$ concentration under $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, which is demonstrated by a gradual color shift toward the red as the concentration of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ increases (Figure 5d,e). Surprisingly, the luminescence color of 1%Ho,Yb
BTC MOFs is close to white, whereas the luminescence color of $x\%\mathrm{Ho}$ ,Yb-BTC MOFs is nearly pure red when the doping concentration of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ is more than $40\%$ .
Encouraged by their excellent multimode luminescent performance, these lanthanide-doped MOFs are investigated for advanced information storage and encryption. As displayed in Figure 6a, six different lanthanide-doped MOFs can produce different emissions under three-mode excitation $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=254$ , 393, and $980\mathrm{nm}$ ), respectively. The six kinds of MOFs can be endowed with six information modules, which are arranged into a specific information Matrix 1 (Figure S21, Supporting Information), and the “A,” “B,” and “C” color information can be output under different excitation wavelength, respectively. Compared with the Matrix 1, the Matrix 2 is only modified by one module (sounded by yellow dotted line). In this case, the information to be output remains the same under 254 and $393{\mathrm{nm}}$ excitation, respectively. While under $980\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, the output informations are different (information “C” and “D”), just like the difference that displayed by Matrix 1 and Matrix 2 (Figure 6b). Furthermore, these lanthanide-doped MOFs are assembled into a barcode for information storage with MOFs I, II, and VI (Figure 6c). The false barcode is read out under 254 and $393\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation. In contrast, under ${980}~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation the true barcode information is output (Figure 6d). Therefore, this study makes a major contribution to research on multimode luminescent MOFs by demonstrating that it is of great potential for optical information storage and encryption applications.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully synthesized lanthanide-doped MOFs with multimodal luminescent properties (both upconversion and down-shifting luminescence). The ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ codoped MOFs emit red light under 254 and $393\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation, and display upconversion emission bands at 497 (derived from $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ ) and $615\mathrm{nm}$ (from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ) upon excitation with $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser. The ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ co-doped MOFs could emit green light under $254\mathrm{nm}$ excitation and exhibit two major upconversion emission bands at 497 (from ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ ) and $545\mathrm{nm}$ (from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ) upon $980\mathrm{nm}$ excitation. Furthermore, the ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ co-doped MOFs, unexpectedly, exhibit near-white upconversion emission when the doping concentration of ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ being $1\%$ . The multimode luminescent MOFs are selected and successfully employed for optical information storage applications. This research sheds new light on detectable multimodal emission under UV lamps (254 and $393~\mathrm{nm}$ ) or $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser. Additionally, this unique optical property is difficult to be imitated, so that it enables these multimodal luminescent MOFs to display excellent potential for advanced information encryption.
